The console flush lock is not waited for for ever

console_ring_try_empty() holds the flush lock while it writes the
characters to the device.  If the device does not take them, the lock is
never released, and every other thread that prints waits on it -- including
the thread that has to drain the ring, which is the only one that can.  The
boot thread, on its way to bsd_init(), prints the copyright, and that is
where it would stop.

So the wait is bounded now.  The ring is a buffer for the device, and
console_write() already writes straight to the device when the ring has no
room; a message that is lost is better than a boot that stops.

diff --git a/osfmk/console/serial_console.c b/osfmk/console/serial_console.c
index 7eccba7..e227243 100644
--- a/osfmk/console/serial_console.c
+++ b/osfmk/console/serial_console.c
@@ -403,7 +403,28 @@ console_ring_try_empty(bool fail_fast)
 		if (__probable(!in_debugger) && fail_fast && !lck_mtx_try_lock(&console_ring.flush_lock)) {
 			return;
 		} else if (__probable(!in_debugger) && !fail_fast) {
-			lck_mtx_lock(&console_ring.flush_lock);
+			/* nio: do not wait for the flush lock for ever.
+			 *
+			 * The lock is held while the characters are written to
+			 * the device, so a device that does not take them keeps
+			 * every other thread waiting here -- and the thread
+			 * that is waiting is the one that has to drain the
+			 * ring.  The run 555 panel ends with the console's
+			 * last line and no bsd_init phase at all, which is
+			 * what a boot thread stuck on this lock looks like.
+			 *
+			 * Give up after a while instead: the ring is a buffer
+			 * for the device, and console_write() already writes
+			 * straight to the device when the ring has no room.
+			 * A message that is lost is better than a boot that
+			 * stops. */
+			unsigned int nio_tries = 0;
+			while (!lck_mtx_try_lock(&console_ring.flush_lock)) {
+				if (++nio_tries > 10000u) {
+					return;
+				}
+				cpu_pause();
+			}
 		}
 
 		if (__probable(!in_debugger)) {
